store("writeDraft",draft);
const directory=load("sourceCheckDir");
const validation = "# Validation of comparison 1

The complete report was read, including all 27 claim-accounting entries, cross-cutting assessment, findings and completion status. The draft's complete headline, standfirst, byline, lead, subheadings, paragraphs, quotation bridges and quotations are covered. The supplied source passages preserve the relevant actors, modifiers, chronology, modal terms, count units and document attribution. The person, number and natural-gender analysis matches the actual prose: no natural-gender assertion is added. The byline has the separately supplied invocation authority. Both direct quotations preserve the complete source answers.

No accounting mismatch was detected. The institution-level attribution of the manager's approval in the standfirst is supported in context by the body's exact attribution. The shortened standfirst description of postponement is supported by the body's full status-review condition. The email context means 'said' does not introduce a spoken scene. The fictional-status sentence rests on the explicit synthetic-material declaration.

F1 accepted. Draft: 'Fenwick staff see which instruments await parts'. Source: 'It says staff could see which instruments were waiting for parts', where 'It' is the workshop's trial summary. The title's direct 'see' does not explicitly preserve the source's reported ability. The report's non-exercised-ability example is compatible with that source. Repair: 'Fenwick says staff could identify instruments awaiting parts'. This attributes the claim to Fenwick and keeps 'could'. In this schedule context 'identify instruments awaiting parts' renders 'see which instruments were waiting for parts', without a claim about physically observing instruments or tracking their movements. Fenwick's own summary supplies the institutional reporting attribution. This is a supported wording of the checker's proposed attribution/modal repair, inside the headline norms.

F2 accepted. Draft: 'After a six-week scheduling trial, Fenwick Instrument Repairs approved continued use for new jobs but postponed adding its backlog.' Source: 'The manager approved continued use for new jobs but postponed adding the backlog until the team had reviewed its status definitions.' The material dates the trial's start and the summary, but not the approval. The report's decision-in-week-five example is not excluded. Repair: 'For new jobs, Fenwick Instrument Repairs approved continued use but postponed adding its backlog.' This removes the unsupported after-trial date relation and preserves the approved scope. The body retains the entire status-review condition.

All other prose is preserved. No translation findings occurred; the source and target quotations are English. A fresh second comparison is required because the first comparison led to prose repairs. No findings or defence will be sent to the second checker.

Completion status: comparison 1 complete and validated. Accepted findings F1 and F2 repaired for comparison 2. No disputed findings or accounting mismatches remain.
";
text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:"python3 - <<'PY'
from pathlib import Path
run_dir = Path("+JSON.stringify(directory)+")
(run_dir / 'draft-2.md').write_text("+JSON.stringify(draft)+", encoding='utf-8')
(run_dir / 'validation-1.md').write_text("+JSON.stringify(validation)+", encoding='utf-8')
print('Saved comparison-1 validation and the complete repaired draft for comparison 2.')
PY",max_output_tokens:1000}));
store("validation1",validation);

